Here's a JavaScript code that removes the `(age X)` pattern from the string:

```javascript
const str = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";

const result = str.replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)/i, "");

console.log(result);
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

## How it works:

| Pattern Part | Description |
|---|---|
| `\s*` | Removes any spaces before `(age X)` |
| `\(` | Matches literal `(` |
| `age` | Matches the word "age" |
| `\s+` | Matches one or more spaces |
| `\d+` | Matches **any number** (7, 25, 100, etc.) |
| `\)` | Matches literal `)` |
| `/i` | Case-insensitive flag |

## Test with different ages:

```javascript
const strings = [
  "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)",
  "july 7 1994 (age 25) London, UK (1994-07-07)",
  "july 7 1994 (age 100) London, UK (1994-07-07)",
];

strings.forEach(str => {
  console.log(str.replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)/i, ""));
});

// Output:
// "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
// "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
// "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

